Z||000000||BAM-BAM||05-13-2001||02:10 PM|||| I have been looking at amplifiers and have decided on a five channel amp to run my system. Now, where I get confused is, in figureing the out/ohms thing. I have four Planet audio 3250X 3.5" speakers in the headrests, two planet audio 4250X 4" in the dash and a Sony L1035 Sub. Will a MTX Blue Thunder Pro 400 run this? I have also bee looking at an Eclipse 3650 5 chennel. How do I tell what the resistance will be? Sorry for the ignorance, but you have to start somewhere. TIA<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000001||Seanh||05-13-2001||03:18 PM||||Alright, that vast majority of speakers are 4ohms each. So, if you have 4 speakers, 2 on each side, and they are wired in series (+ from stereo to + of 1 speaker, - of that speaker to + of next speaker, and - of that speaker to - of stereo channel) then the total impedence for that channel will the 8 ohms. So, chances are your amp is rated at 4ohms per side, so 8 ohms is twice 4 ohms, so the power will be cut in half from the 4ohm rateing. Your 4" speaks in the dash are 4ohms each, so they will get the full output of the amp if it is rated at 4ohms. I believe that amp is capable of running 2 ohms stereo, so you could wire the headrest pairs in perallel. Where the + from stereo(or amp channel) goes to the + on both left or right speakers, and same with -. That would create a 2ohm load for each speaker pair, so you would get the rated power for 2ohms, or technically twice the output of the 4 ohm rating. Then that would be the best solution because all your speakers would get the same amount of power. Your dash speakers would be 4ohms each and your headset speakers (each pair, side) would get twice that power, however that will be divided between the two speakers, so they all get the same power. Then use the last channel to run the subwoofer.<P>The MTX would give all 6 speakers 25w RMS and 65w MAX power, and the subwoofer (if 4ohm) 100w RMS, or if 2ohm then 200w RMS power. Pretty descent, however I think the speaker power is kinda low, but then again, MTX's are underrated so they will actually put out more power then that. I am not familiar with that Eclipse off hand, but Eclipse makes awesome quality products all around.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||05-13-2001||04:40 PM||||jl audio makes one that sounds like it would be good, spec wise. i may look for a couple for my truck (after i look for the money for them) the output sounds like it would be good for a fiero system.<BR>5 channels, 2x100, 2x25, 1x250. the only flaw...about $800.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||05-13-2001||06:03 PM||||  Thanks Seanh, that's what I was thinking although I wasn't quite sure. The MTX Amp I am looking at is just as you said, 25W RMS @ 4ohms. I saw the cetification sheet for this amp and though it is rated for 25W it's actual output, RMS, is 53W. So, that being the case, I think it will do the trick. Thanks for your help, I appriciate it alot.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||6
